Answer: A class name should start with an uppercase letter. If it contains multiple words, every word should start with an uppercase letter. For example:

```java
class StudentApp {
}
```

Here, StudentApp follows the class naming convention.

Answer: The logic is written inside the main() method when the program is being executed as a Java application. The main method is written as:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
}
```

The class containing the main() method follows the class naming convention.

Answer: No. Two local variables cannot have the same name in the same scope. For example:

```java
int temp = 10;
int temp = 20;
```

This produces a duplicate local variable compilation error.

Answer: Yes. An underscore can be used inside an integer literal to improve readability. For example:

```java
int a = 45;
int b = 4_5;
```

Both represent the value 45. The underscore can be placed between digits, but it cannot be placed at the beginning or end of the literal.

Answer: When an integer literal is prefixed with 0, it is treated as an octal number. For example:

```java
int a = 45;
int b = 045;
System.out.println(a);
System.out.println(b);
```

Output: 45 37 045 is an octal literal, so its decimal value is 37.

Answer: When an integer literal is prefixed with 0x, it is treated as a hexadecimal number. For example:

```java
int i = 0x45;
System.out.println(i);
```

Output: 69 The hexadecimal value 45 represents decimal 69.

Answer: When an integer literal is prefixed with 0b, it is treated as a binary number. For example:

```java
int d = 0B1001100;
System.out.println(d);
```

Output: 76 The binary value is converted to its decimal value when printed.

Answer: Scientific notation is another way of representing a real number by moving the decimal point and specifying the number of positions moved using E. The following examples represent the same value:

```java
float A = 1234.56F;
float B = 12.3456E+2F;
float C = 12345.6E-1F;
```

All three represent 1234.56.
